C(279,[468],[282,284,290],'E2-27,126-156,401-426; IX3.5,3.8pp350,353;9.2p419','V and Vperp; unipotent echelon<=2.','The semistable/inertia criteria imply (sigma-1)(tau-1)=0 on the appropriate Tate module and the correct inclusion Vperp⊂V. The printed text contains direction/inclusion tensions.','published equivalences; wording alarms','E','Original SGA7IX3.5(ii)p350 prints V⊂Vperp while its proof ends with Vperp⊂V; Corollary3.8p353 labels an implication opposite to its written argument. These exact source tensions were visually/locally checked.','The standard criterion is retained with explicit reading alarms. It would be wrong to silently repair only the archive transcription.',['late_SGA7IX'],['L18'],proof='Targeted consistency check of statement, proof endpoint and transvection product; no full independent proof of the semistable criterion.')
C(280,[508],[300],'E710-730; IX12.8.3p478','representability admitted; Mumford/Raynaud; Artin algebraic spaces.','The relative Picard functor is represented under the source assumptions; the proof excerpt explicitly relies on then-unpublished representability input and allows algebraic spaces in the broader setting. Formal smoothness uses the curve H² obstruction vanishing.','published source admission and proof sketch','B','Stacks0D24 Proposition99.11.8 proves Picard algebraic-space representability for flat proper finitely presented X/B with O_T≅f_T*O_XT universally. This is a precise modern theorem; it does not automatically give a scheme in every case.','Representability is established under those exact hypotheses. The source’s historical attribution and scheme/algebraic-space distinction are preserved.',['late_SGA7IX','late_Stacks0D24','late_Artin1969'],['L19'],proof='Exact modern theorem and proof read; obstruction vanishing is standard dimension-one background.',confidence='high')
C(281,[509],[292,294],'E471-507,518-563','bounded partial sums; decreasing c_n; remainder estimate.','Dirichlet convergence follows for c_n decreasing to0 and bounded partial sums of u_n. A bound |S_n|<=M gives a general tail bound2M c_(n+1), not necessarily M c_(n+1).','exercise with incomplete hypotheses','E','Author lecture source Gressman, Advanced Analysis, Infinite Series, states the standard Dirichlet test and summation-by-parts proof. The manuscript exercise is elementary background unrelated to the live de Rham sequence.','The convergence theorem is correct; the sharper constant needs a bound on interval sums. u=(-1,2,-1,0,...) and c_n=1/n gives R1=2/3>1/2.',['late_GressmanSeries'],['L20'],proof='proofs.md §13 independent summation-by-parts check.',computation='late_computations_output.json elementary_checks.Dirichlet_tail_bound_counterexample.',confidence='medium')
C(282,[509],[292,294],'E471-507,518-563','periodic u_n; sum over one period.','A periodic sequence has bounded partial sums iff its period mean is zero. With positive decreasing c_n→0 and divergent sum c_n, the weighted periodic series converges exactly when that mean is zero.','exercise claim','E','The opened Dirichlet-test source gives the convergence input; decomposition into mean plus a zero-mean periodic sequence proves the exact criterion.','Correct with the positivity, decay and divergence hypotheses stated.',['late_GressmanSeries'],['L20'],proof='proofs.md §13 independent period-block argument.')
C(283,[509],[294],'E518-563','sum z^n; bounded away from1.','Geometric partial sums satisfy z(1-z^N)/(1-z); for |z|<=1,z≠1 they are bounded by2|z|/|1-z|, uniformly on sets bounded away from1.','exercise computation','E','Dirichlet/summation-by-parts source supplies the standard application; the geometric identity is independently checked.','Correct. Uniformity cannot include an uncontrolled approach to z=1.',['late_GressmanSeries'],['L20'],proof='proofs.md §13 geometric-series identity and bound.')
C(284,[471,438],[275],'D1584-1604; earlier-note D#75','weighted binomial product factors.','Compatible weighted products whose comparison factors are binom(r+s,r), for all sufficiently large r,s, determine the ordinary integral cup product because those integers have gcd1.','compatibility sketched on pages; gcd deduction belongs to earlier note','B','Cartan1976 §7p271 explicitly states the gcd1 argument and cup-product recovery. Thus the earlier-run Bezout example6*6−35=1 is a correct illustration of an already published deduction, not an unpursued new implication.','Established with the cofinal family of weights and natural comparison identifications. A single weight pair would not suffice.',['late_Cartan1976'],['L01'],proof='Primewise no-carry choice r=ell^a,s=ell^b shows gcd1; exact Bezout example checked.',computation='late_computations_output.json product_bezout.',date='Grothendieck lecture12Dec1975; Cartan publicationDec1976.',origin='His weighted compatibility; earlier-run deduction; exact published antecedent identified')
C(285,[489],[324],'E1922; refined statement for C255','No verbatim source statement; refinement of his question.','If h is commutative, A is a flat counital cocommutative h-coalgebra and (B,J,gamma) a PD h-algebra, Hom_h(A,J) has canonical natural convolution divided powers gamma_n(f)=e_n Gamma^n(f) delta_n, with delta_n the inverse-invariant-tensor lift of the n-fold coproduct.','our refined theorem, not attributed verbatim to the pages','C','SGA4XVII5.5.2.4–5, originalp374, gives flatness of Gamma and Gamma^n(A)≅TS^n(A). Roby1963/1968 and Stacks07GK supply the polynomial/PD identities. The exact assembled Hom theorem was not found verbatim; no novelty claim is made.','Proved in the memo. J need not be flat: only A and Gamma^q A are tested through injective tensor maps, and evaluation Gamma^n J→J follows from the free PD universal property.',['late_SGA4XVII','late_Lundkvist2008','late_Roby1963','late_Roby1968','late_Stacks07GK'],['L13'],proof='proofs.md §11 verifies addition, scalar, product and iteration using split/block coproduct identities.',origin='Our reconstruction of a correct sufficient-hypothesis version')
C(286,[430,484],[277,299],'D1695-1705; E688-707','No separate verbatim source statement; coefficient presentation completion.','For nonnegative Gamma_k(T)-modules, the left coefficient adjoint is the explicit binomial quotient L and is exact; for negative tail degrees the coefficient representing group is Q. These are distinct covariant/contravariant coefficient effects.','our completed reconstruction of two incomplete calculations','C','Searches across Roby/SGA4, Gabriel localization, Kelly and later numerical/binomial homotopy sources did not identify a theorem stating this exact pair of cutoff formulas. The proof is elementary and does not establish priority.','Proved with prime-local cofinal rows. The negative term is Hom_Z(Q,M), while L is a filtered-colimit-style left adjoint; confusing them reverses variance.',['late_Kelly1982','late_SGA4XVII','late_Gabriel1962'],['L05'],proof='proofs.md §§5,8.',computation='late_computations_output.json binomial_rigidity,negative_tail_relation.',origin='Our re-proof/completion of earlier-run ideas')
C(287,[469],[256,257,273],'D888-959,1552-1569; reconstructed quotient','No verbatim source statement; replacement quotient.','Let T be the localizing Serre closure of bounded nonnegative Gamma_k(T)-modules. For Q the quotient and R its section functor, the coefficient embedding satisfies R R Q(iM)≅iM in the bounded-below derived setting.','our replacement theorem; changes the literal negligible class','C','Gabriel/Stacks02MN give localization under a specified localizing class; the exact left adjoint L and injective-local argument supply this special recovery. No identification of T with the manuscript elementwise class is claimed.','The precise replacement is proved. This does not prove the manuscript colimit formula or strong monoidal recovery for an unspecified C_infty.',['late_Gabriel1962','late_Stacks02MN','late_Stacks015Z','late_Stacks015M'],['L06','L07'],proof='proofs.md §7: L kills T, iI is injective and T-local, Q(iI) injective, resolution computes the derived unit.',origin='Our explicit repaired formulation')

